deepseek rep1 18/48 3hop1__28404_160713_77246: 'Let\'s break down the question step by step.\n\n**Step 1: "the country that disavowed the Taliban"**  \nFrom passage 9: "particular neighboring Pakistan, which has disowned its earlier support for the Taliban"  \n→ This country is **Pakistan**.\n\n**Step 2: "when the country that disavowed the Taliban was created"**  \nFrom passage 6: "The two self-governing countries of Pakistan and India legally came into existence at midnight on 14–15 August 1947."  \n→ Pakistan was created in **1947**.\n\n**Step 3: "what became India when [Pakistan] was created"**  \nFrom passage 6: "The Dominion of India is today the Republic of India" and "India was formed out of the majority Hindu regions of British India"  \n→ What became India at that time was the **Dominion of India** (or simply India).\n\n**Step 4: "a majority religion in what became India"**  \nFrom passage 14: "79.8% of the population of India practice Hinduism."  \n→ Majority religion is **Hinduism**.\n\n**Step 5: "the word that is also a majority religion"**  \nThe word is **Hindu**.\n\n**Step 6: "the meaning of the word ... in the Arabic dictionary"**  \nFrom passage 16: "The Arabic equivalent Al-Hind likewise referred to the country of India."  \nBut the question asks for the meaning of the word "Hindu" in the Arabic dictionary. Passage 16 says the term Hindu is derived from Sindhu meaning "a large body of water", covering "river, ocean". It also says the Arabic equivalent Al-Hind referred to the country of India.  \nHowever, the question specifically asks: "What is the meaning of the word that is also a majority religion in what became India when the country that disavowed the Taliban was created in the Arabic dictionary?"\n\nThis is a bit convoluted. Let\'s parse:  \n- "the word that is also a majority religion" = Hindu  \n- "in what became India when [Pakistan] was created" = Dominion of India  \n- "in the Arabic dictionary" = Arabic equivalent Al-Hind referred to the country of India.\n\nBut the meaning of "Hindu" in Arabic? The passage says Al-Hind referred to the country of India. So the meaning is **India**.\n\nBut wait—the question says "the meaning of the word that is also a majority religion ... in the Arabic dictionary". That could mean: what does the Arabic dictionary say the word "Hindu" means? Passage 16: "The Arabic equivalent Al-Hind likewise referred to the country of India." So the meaning is **India**.\n\nHowever, the question might be asking for the meaning of "Hindu" itself, which passage 16 says is "a large body of water" covering "river, ocean". But that\'s from Sanskrit, not Arabic.\n\nThe phrase "in the Arabic dictionary" likely points to the Arabic equivalent Al-Hind meaning India. So the meaning is **India**.\n\nBut let\'s check: the question asks "What is the meaning of the word that is also a majority religion in what became India when the country that disavowed the Taliban was created in the Arabic dictionary?"  \nThe word is Hindu. In Arabic, Al-Hind means India. So the meaning is India.\n\nBut is that the meaning of the word "Hindu" in Arabic? The passage says the Arabic equivalent Al-Hind referred to the country of India. So yes, the meaning is India.\n\nHowever, the question might be asking for the meaning of the word "Hindu" as given in the passage: "a large body of water", covering "river, ocean". But that\'s not Arabic.\n\nGiven the phrasing "in the Arabic dictionary", it\'s likely asking for the Arabic meaning, which is India.\n\nBut let\'s re-read: "What is the meaning of the word that is also a majority religion in what became India when the country that disavowed the Taliban was created in the Arabic dictionary?"\n\nThis is grammatically awkward. It could be: What is the meaning of [the word that is also a majority religion in what became India when the country that disavowed the Taliban was created] in the Arabic dictionary?\n\nThe word is Hindu. In the Arabic dictionary, what does Hindu 